@meta
id: 27-01
title: Flip a switch
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: set-option
par: 28
minutes: 4
vscode: Settings, search for "insert spaces", untick a box
open: config.ts
vimrc: set noexpandtab

@brief
Every lesson so far has handed you a vim that behaves. This chapter is
where you take the controls.

Vim's behaviour lives in options: a few hundred named switches, dials and
lists. Most of them are booleans, and a boolean option is on or off.

This session has the Tab key set to insert a real tab character. The house
style for this repo is two spaces, and the three fields in `config.ts` are
flat against the left margin where they should be indented one level.
Indent them the way you always have and you will get tab characters, and
the file will not match.

So change the option first, then indent.

The option is called `expandtab`: when it is on, anything that would insert
a tab inserts spaces instead.

@keys
| `:set expandtab` | turn the option on |
| `:set noexpandtab` | turn it off — the `no` prefix is how you say off |
| `:set expandtab?` | ask what it is right now |
| `:set et` | every option has a short name too |

@hints
`>>` is doing exactly what you asked. The problem is what vim thinks a
unit of indent is made of. Change that, then indent.
--
The option is `expandtab`. Turning an option on is `:set` and its name.
--
`:set expandtab` then Enter, then indent the three fields as usual:
`2G` to the first one, `3>>` for three lines.

@solution
    :set expandtab<Enter>  2G  3>>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes, and fourteen of them are the option.

Three things worth keeping:

`:set name` turns a boolean on. `:set noname` turns it off. There is no
`=true`, no quotes, no punctuation — the presence or absence of `no` is
the whole syntax.

Every option has a long name and a short one. `expandtab` is `et`,
`shiftwidth` is `sw`, `ignorecase` is `ic`. Type the short one when you
are poking around in a live session; write the long one in a config file,
where somebody else has to read it in a year's time.

And `:set expandtab?` with a question mark *asks* instead of setting. That
matters more than it looks: `:set expandtab` and `:set expandtab?` are
different commands, and typing the first when you meant the second is how
people silently change the thing they were trying to inspect.

@start config.ts
export const database = {
host: 'localhost',
port: 5432,
poolSize: 10,
};

@expected config.ts
export const database = {
  host: 'localhost',
  port: 5432,
  poolSize: 10,
};
